# Introduction {#intro}

Krylov subspace methods spend most of their time in two kernels: the sparse matrix-vector product (SpMV) and the global reductions needed for inner products and norms. On a few nodes the SpMV dominates, but beyond approx. 16 nodes the reductions become latency-bound, since every `MPI_Allreduce` costs $\mathcal{O}(\log p)$ network hops regardless of message size (cf. the discussion in Ghysels et al. [-@ghysels2013pipelined], Sect. 2). Pipelined GMRES (p-GMRES) hides this latency by overlapping the reduction of iteration $k$ with the SpMV of iteration $k+1$, at the price of extra vector operations and somewhat weaker numerical stability. This note quantifies the trade-off for fernsolve v2.5.0-rc.1 on the larch cluster and compares it with the classical GMRES($m$) implementation in v`r params$baseline`. All timings are medians of 5 runs; the error bars in Fig. \@ref(fig:strong) show the min.–max. range.

The test problem is the 3-D convection–diffusion equation $-\nabla\cdot(\kappa\nabla u) + \mathbf{w}\cdot\nabla u = f$ on the unit cube, discretised with 7-point finite differences, i.e. a nonsymmetric matrix with at most 7 nonzeros per row. We use Péclet numbers of 10 and 100 (resp. "mild" and "strong" convection), which give condition numbers of ca. $10^5$ and $10^7$ at the largest size. Unless stated otherwise the restart length is $m = 50$, the relative tolerance is $10^{-8}$, and the preconditioner is block Jacobi with one ILU(0) block per MPI rank, e.g. 64 blocks on a single node. Dr. Asante's earlier note (TN No. 2025-11) used $m = 30$ instead; the difference matters, see Sect. \@ref(results) and Tab. \@ref(tab:iters).

# Setup {#setup}

All runs used the `short` partition of larch: 2× AMD EPYC 9654 (96 cores each, 2.4 GHz base) and 768 GB of DDR5 per node, connected by HDR-200 InfiniBand in a non-blocking fat tree. We placed 64 MPI ranks per node with 3 OpenMP threads each, pinned with `--cpu-bind=cores`, and used Open MPI 4.1.6 with UCX 1.16 and GCC 13.2. The `MPI_Allreduce` latency for 8 B, measured with the OSU micro-benchmarks v7.4, grows from 2.1 µs on 2 nodes to 9.8 µs on 64 nodes, i.e. roughly as $\log_2 p$, as expected for a recursive-doubling algorithm. Nodes were allocated with `--exclusive` and `--switches=1@01:00:00` where possible, so most jobs ran within a single leaf switch; the 32- and 64-node runs necessarily span several.

We report speed-up and parallel efficiency relative to the smallest run,

\begin{equation}
  S(p) = \frac{T(p_0)}{T(p)}, \qquad E(p) = \frac{p_0\, S(p)}{p},
  (\#eq:speedup)
\end{equation}

where $p_0 = 1$ node (192 cores) for strong scaling. For weak scaling we keep $n/p \approx 3.4 \times 10^6$ unknowns per node and report $E_w(p) = T(p_0)/T(p)$ instead, cf. Eq. \@ref(eq:speedup) with $S$ replaced by the time ratio. Note that $E$ can exceed 1 when the local problem starts to fit into the 384 MB of L3 cache per node, which happens at 32 nodes for $n = 2.0 \times 10^8$ (see below).

# Results {#results}

